Persistent nasal congestion, difficulty breathing through the nose, reduced sense of smell, and recurring sinus infections can significantly affect a person's quality of life. While these symptoms are often associated with allergies or common colds, they may sometimes indicate a condition known as nasal polyps.
Nasal polyps are soft, non-cancerous growths that develop in the lining of the nose or paranasal sinuses. They are commonly associated with long-term inflammation and may occur alongside chronic rhinosinusitis, allergic conditions, or asthma. Small nasal polyps may cause few noticeable symptoms, while larger or multiple growths can obstruct the nasal passages and interfere with normal breathing.
Although nasal polyps are generally benign, they should not be ignored when symptoms persist. The condition can affect sleep, concentration, exercise tolerance, taste, smell, and overall wellbeing. Some people experience repeated episodes of nasal blockage despite using ordinary cold medicines or allergy treatments.
The good news is that several nasal polyp treatment options are available. Depending on the severity of the condition, treatment may include saline nasal irrigation, corticosteroid nasal sprays, selected oral medications, biologic therapies, or endoscopic sinus surgery. The appropriate approach depends on the underlying inflammation, the size and location of the polyps, other medical conditions, and the patient's response to previous treatment.
This guide explains nasal polyp symptoms, possible causes, risk factors, diagnosis, and available treatment options. It also discusses nasal polyps and allergies, their relationship with asthma, recovery after surgery, and strategies for reducing the likelihood of recurrence.
Medical disclaimer: This article provides general educational information and does not replace an individual assessment by a qualified healthcare professional. Persistent nasal symptoms, unexplained one-sided blockage, or changes in vision require appropriate medical evaluation.
What Are Nasal Polyps?
Nasal polyps are soft, usually painless, non-cancerous growths that develop from the inflamed mucous membrane lining the nose and sinuses.
The nose and paranasal sinuses are lined with tissue that produces mucus and helps trap dust, allergens, and microorganisms. This lining also helps warm and humidify the air a person breathes.
When inflammation continues for a prolonged period, the lining may become swollen. In some individuals, this persistent inflammation contributes to the formation of polyp-like growths.
Nasal polyps may develop in one or both nasal passages. They are frequently associated with chronic rhinosinusitis, particularly chronic rhinosinusitis with nasal polyps.
A person may have a few small polyps without significant symptoms. Larger polyps or multiple growths may block airflow, interfere with sinus drainage, and reduce the sense of smell.
Nasal polyps are not the same as ordinary mucus, swollen nasal tissue during a cold, or enlarged adenoids. They are distinct growths that a healthcare professional can assess through a nasal examination and, when necessary, additional investigations.
Are Nasal Polyps Cancerous?
Most nasal polyps are benign and are not cancerous. However, not every growth inside the nose is a nasal polyp.
A persistent growth affecting only one side of the nose, especially if accompanied by bleeding, facial numbness, severe pain, or other unusual symptoms, requires medical assessment to rule out other conditions.
A clinician may recommend additional imaging or tissue examination if the appearance is atypical or there is concern about another diagnosis.
It is important not to assume that every nasal growth is harmless without appropriate evaluation.
Types of Nasal Polyps
Nasal polyps are generally classified according to their location, appearance, and associated conditions.
1. Bilateral Nasal Polyps
Bilateral nasal polyps develop in both nasal passages. They are commonly associated with chronic inflammation and chronic rhinosinusitis.
People with bilateral polyps may experience persistent nasal blockage, reduced smell, postnasal drip, and pressure around the nose or forehead.
Treatment usually focuses on controlling the underlying inflammation and maintaining sinus drainage.
2. Antrochoanal Polyps
Antrochoanal polyps usually originate in the maxillary sinus, which is located in the cheek region, and extend towards the back of the nasal cavity.
They are more frequently identified in children and young adults than the typical bilateral inflammatory polyps seen in adults.
An antrochoanal polyp may cause one-sided nasal obstruction, snoring, mouth breathing, or a sensation of blockage at the back of the nose.
Treatment often involves surgery to remove the polyp and address its origin. The precise approach depends on its size, location, and extent.
3. Inflammatory Nasal Polyps
Inflammatory nasal polyps are associated with prolonged inflammation of the nasal and sinus lining. They may occur alongside allergies, asthma, or other inflammatory conditions, although not every patient has an identifiable allergy.
These polyps can recur even after treatment because the underlying inflammatory tendency may persist.
Long-term management may therefore involve both treating existing polyps and controlling the inflammation that contributes to their development.
Common Nasal Polyp Symptoms
Nasal polyp symptoms vary according to the number, size, and location of the growths. Small polyps may remain unnoticed, while larger ones can cause persistent problems.
Recognising the common symptoms of nasal polyps can help people determine when to seek medical advice.
1. Persistent Nasal Congestion
Nasal congestion is one of the most common symptoms of nasal polyps.
People may feel that their nose is blocked even when they do not have a cold. The blockage may affect one or both sides and can remain present for weeks or months.
Some individuals describe the sensation as an inability to draw enough air through the nose.
Nasal congestion can also result from allergic rhinitis, a deviated nasal septum, enlarged nasal turbinates, or chronic sinusitis. A clinical assessment helps distinguish between these causes.
2. Difficulty Breathing Through the Nose
Larger nasal polyps can physically obstruct the nasal passages, making nasal breathing difficult.
People may begin breathing through their mouths, particularly during sleep or exercise.
Persistent nasal obstruction can interfere with comfortable breathing and may contribute to snoring or disturbed sleep.
If nasal blockage is severe, persistent, or predominantly one-sided, it should be assessed rather than treated indefinitely with over-the-counter decongestants.
3. Reduced Sense of Smell
A reduced sense of smell, medically known as hyposmia, is a characteristic symptom of nasal polyps.
The sense of smell depends on airborne molecules reaching specialised sensory cells high inside the nasal cavity. Inflammation and polyps can interfere with this process.
Some people experience a partial reduction in smell, while others lose their sense of smell almost completely.
Because much of what people perceive as flavour depends on smell, reduced olfactory function may also make food seem less enjoyable.
A sudden change in smell can have other causes, including viral infections. Persistent or unexplained smell loss should be evaluated by a healthcare professional.
4. Reduced Sense of Taste
People with nasal polyps may report that food tastes bland or that familiar flavours are less noticeable.
This is often related to the reduced sense of smell rather than a problem with the taste buds themselves.
If taste changes persist, a clinician can assess whether nasal inflammation, infection, medication, or another condition may be contributing.
5. Postnasal Drip
Postnasal drip occurs when mucus drains from the back of the nose into the throat.
It may cause a sensation of mucus running down the throat, frequent throat clearing, coughing, or irritation.
Postnasal drip can occur with nasal polyps, chronic rhinosinusitis, allergies, and other nasal conditions.
Although it may be uncomfortable, the symptom alone does not confirm the presence of nasal polyps.
6. Facial Pressure or Headache
Some people with nasal polyps and chronic rhinosinusitis experience pressure around the cheeks, forehead, or eyes.
However, nasal polyps do not always cause facial pain, and facial pressure is not specific to sinus disease.
Migraine, tension-type headaches, dental problems, and other conditions can produce similar symptoms.
A healthcare professional should assess persistent or severe headaches, especially if they occur with fever, swelling around the eyes, visual changes, or neurological symptoms.
7. Frequent Sinus Symptoms
Nasal polyps may occur with chronic rhinosinusitis, a condition involving prolonged inflammation of the nose and sinuses.
Affected individuals may experience nasal blockage, discharge, reduced smell, or facial pressure for an extended period.
In adults, chronic rhinosinusitis is generally considered when appropriate symptoms persist for at least 12 weeks, although diagnosis also depends on clinical findings.
Not everyone with recurring sinus symptoms has nasal polyps, and not everyone with nasal polyps experiences repeated bacterial infections.
8. Snoring and Disturbed Sleep
Blocked nasal passages may contribute to snoring, mouth breathing, and disrupted sleep.
Some people wake with a dry mouth or feel less refreshed in the morning.
Persistent sleep problems should be discussed with a healthcare professional. Nasal polyps can contribute to obstruction, but sleep-disordered breathing may have additional causes that require separate assessment.
9. Thick Nasal Discharge
Some people with nasal polyps experience a runny nose or thick nasal discharge.
The discharge may be clear, white, or coloured, depending on the underlying condition.
Coloured mucus alone does not prove that a bacterial infection is present. Symptoms, examination findings, duration, and clinical history help guide diagnosis and treatment.
10. Pressure in the Ears or Throat Discomfort
Inflammation affecting the upper airway may coexist with ear pressure, throat irritation, or frequent throat clearing.
These symptoms have multiple possible causes and should not automatically be attributed to nasal polyps.
If ear pain, hearing changes, persistent throat symptoms, or swallowing difficulties occur, medical evaluation may be appropriate.
What Causes Nasal Polyps?
The exact cause of nasal polyps is not fully understood. However, persistent inflammation of the nasal and sinus lining is strongly associated with their development.
Several factors may contribute to this inflammatory process.
1. Chronic Rhinosinusitis
Chronic rhinosinusitis is one of the most important conditions associated with nasal polyps.
In this condition, the lining of the nose and sinuses remains inflamed for an extended period. Swelling, mucus changes, and impaired sinus drainage may occur.
In some individuals, chronic inflammation leads to the formation of polyps that further obstruct normal airflow and drainage.
Treatment often requires a long-term plan rather than relying solely on medication for occasional symptom relief.
2. Allergic Rhinitis
Allergic rhinitis occurs when the immune system reacts to allergens such as dust mites, pollen, mould, or animal dander.
Typical symptoms include sneezing, itching, a runny nose, and congestion.
Allergic rhinitis may coexist with nasal polyps, but allergies are not the cause of every case. Some people with nasal polyps do not have a demonstrable allergy.
When both conditions are present, managing the allergic symptoms may improve overall nasal comfort, although it may not eliminate established polyps.
3. Asthma
Asthma is a chronic inflammatory condition affecting the airways in the lungs.
Asthma and nasal polyps can occur together, particularly in people with certain patterns of airway inflammation.
Patients with both conditions may experience nasal blockage, reduced smell, wheezing, cough, or breathing difficulties.
Coordinated management is important because treating only the nasal symptoms may leave asthma insufficiently controlled, and vice versa.
4. Sensitivity to Aspirin and Other Non-Steroidal Anti-Inflammatory Drugs
Some people with asthma and nasal polyps have a condition known as aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease, or AERD.
It is characterised by respiratory reactions to aspirin and certain other non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, together with asthma and chronic rhinosinusitis with nasal polyps.
Symptoms may include nasal congestion, wheezing, and respiratory worsening after exposure to particular medicines.
People who suspect this condition should seek specialist advice. They should not deliberately take a suspected trigger medicine to test their reaction.
Do not stop prescribed medication without speaking to the prescribing clinician.
5. Immune and Inflammatory Factors
Changes in immune responses and inflammatory signalling appear to contribute to the development of some nasal polyps.
The specific processes vary among individuals. Researchers continue to study why certain patients develop severe or recurrent disease while others experience milder symptoms.
This is one reason why treatment response can differ from person to person.
6. Other Medical Conditions
Certain conditions, including cystic fibrosis, can be associated with nasal polyps, particularly in children and younger individuals.
A clinician may consider additional evaluation when polyps occur at an unusually young age, are recurrent, or appear alongside other suggestive symptoms.
However, most adults with nasal polyps do not have cystic fibrosis.
Who Is at Greater Risk of Developing Nasal Polyps?
Nasal polyps can develop in different age groups, but some people have a higher likelihood of experiencing them.
Risk factors and associated conditions include:
•	Chronic rhinosinusitis.
•	Asthma.
•	Aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease.
•	Allergic rhinitis or other allergic conditions.
•	Certain genetic or inflammatory conditions.
•	A history of persistent nasal inflammation.
Nasal polyps are more commonly recognised in adults than in young children.
When nasal polyps are identified in a child, the clinician may consider whether further investigation is needed to identify an underlying condition.
Having one or more risk factors does not mean a person will necessarily develop nasal polyps.
How Are Nasal Polyps Diagnosed?
Accurate diagnosis is important because nasal congestion, smell loss, and sinus symptoms can occur in several conditions.
An ear, nose, and throat specialist, commonly called an ENT specialist, may diagnose nasal polyps using a combination of medical history, nasal examination, and selected investigations.
1. Medical History
The clinician may ask about:
•	How long nasal congestion has been present.
•	Whether the blockage affects one or both sides.
•	Changes in the sense of smell or taste.
•	Nasal discharge and postnasal drip.
•	Facial pain or pressure.
•	Previous sinus infections or nasal surgery.
•	History of asthma or allergies.
•	Reactions to aspirin or other anti-inflammatory medicines.
•	Current medications and previous treatment response.
•	The effect of symptoms on sleep and everyday activities.
These details help establish whether the symptoms are consistent with nasal polyps or another condition.
2. Nasal Examination
A clinician may inspect the inside of the nose using a light and an appropriate examination instrument.
This can help identify swelling, discharge, structural abnormalities, or visible polyps.
Small or deeper polyps may not be fully visible during a basic examination, so further evaluation may be necessary.
3. Nasal Endoscopy
Nasal endoscopy is an important diagnostic tool for evaluating suspected nasal polyps.
During the procedure, the clinician uses a thin instrument with a light and camera to examine the nasal passages and accessible sinus openings.
The examination can help identify the presence, location, and extent of polyps and assess other signs of inflammation.
It may also help distinguish polyps from other causes of nasal obstruction.
Nasal endoscopy is usually performed in an outpatient setting. A numbing spray or decongestant may be used when appropriate to improve comfort and visibility.
The clinician can explain what to expect before the procedure.
4. CT Scan of the Sinuses
A computed tomography scan, commonly known as a CT scan, produces detailed images of the sinuses.
It may be recommended when chronic rhinosinusitis is suspected, symptoms persist despite treatment, surgery is being considered, or the extent of sinus involvement needs clarification.
A CT scan can show sinus inflammation, blocked drainage pathways, anatomical variations, and other relevant findings.
It is not necessary for every person with suspected nasal polyps. The decision depends on the clinical assessment.
5. Allergy Testing
Allergy testing may be considered when the medical history suggests allergic rhinitis or another allergic condition that could affect management.
Testing may involve skin-prick testing or selected blood tests for allergen-specific antibodies.
A positive allergy test must be interpreted alongside symptoms and exposure history. It does not automatically establish that an allergy is responsible for all nasal symptoms.
6. Additional Investigations
Additional tests may be recommended in selected cases.
For example, clinicians may investigate underlying conditions when nasal polyps occur in a child, are unusually recurrent, or are associated with other relevant clinical features.
If a growth appears atypical or is present on only one side, further assessment may be needed to rule out other causes.
Biopsy or tissue examination may be appropriate when the diagnosis is uncertain or another type of growth is suspected.
Available Nasal Polyp Treatment Options
Nasal polyp treatment aims to reduce inflammation, relieve obstruction, improve smell, support sinus drainage, and reduce recurrence.
The best treatment depends on symptom severity, the extent of the polyps, associated conditions, previous treatment response, and individual health considerations.
Some patients improve with medication alone. Others require surgery or specialist therapies.
1. Saline Nasal Irrigation
Saline nasal irrigation involves rinsing the nasal passages with a saltwater solution.
It may help remove mucus, allergens, and inflammatory debris while improving nasal comfort.
Saline irrigation is commonly used as part of treatment for chronic rhinosinusitis, including cases associated with nasal polyps.
For safe irrigation, use commercially prepared sterile saline or prepare the solution using water that is distilled, sterile, or previously boiled and cooled. Do not use untreated tap water for nasal irrigation.
Clean and dry the irrigation device according to the manufacturer's instructions.
Saline irrigation may help manage symptoms, but it does not reliably eliminate established nasal polyps on its own.
2. Corticosteroid Nasal Sprays
Intranasal corticosteroid sprays are a key treatment for nasal polyps.
These medicines reduce inflammation inside the nose and may improve congestion, smell, and other symptoms. Examples include sprays containing medicines such as mometasone, fluticasone, or budesonide.
The appropriate product and dose depend on the individual's age, medical history, and clinical assessment.
Unlike decongestant sprays, corticosteroid nasal sprays are generally intended for regular use as directed rather than providing immediate relief within minutes.
Benefits may take days or weeks to become noticeable, and consistent use is often important.
Correct spray technique can improve delivery to the nasal lining and reduce irritation. A clinician or pharmacist can demonstrate how to use the spray properly.
Possible side effects include nasal dryness, irritation, and nosebleeds. Seek advice if side effects persist or symptoms worsen.
3. Corticosteroid Nasal Drops or Specialised Delivery Methods
In selected cases, clinicians may recommend corticosteroid drops or other delivery methods that improve medication access to affected areas.
The choice depends on the extent of disease, nasal anatomy, previous surgery, and the patient's ability to use the treatment safely.
These methods should be used according to specialist instructions.
4. Oral Corticosteroids
Short courses of oral corticosteroids may be prescribed for selected patients with severe nasal polyp symptoms or significant inflammation.
They can reduce swelling and sometimes produce a temporary improvement in nasal breathing and smell.
However, oral corticosteroids can cause important side effects, especially with repeated or prolonged use. These may include raised blood glucose, increased blood pressure, mood changes, sleep disturbance, infection risk, and bone-related complications.
For this reason, they are generally reserved for specific situations rather than used as a routine long-term treatment.
Do not start, repeat, or discontinue a prescribed corticosteroid course without appropriate medical guidance.
5. Treatment for Allergies
When allergic rhinitis occurs alongside nasal polyps, treating the allergy may help reduce sneezing, itching, and watery nasal discharge.
Depending on the symptoms, treatment may include appropriate antihistamines, corticosteroid nasal sprays, or allergen-specific management.
Antihistamines may be useful for allergic symptoms but do not usually shrink established nasal polyps as effectively as corticosteroid-based treatment.
Allergy immunotherapy may be appropriate for selected patients with clinically significant allergies. It is not a universal treatment for nasal polyps and should be considered following specialist assessment.
6. Biologic Medicines
Biologic therapies are targeted medicines that affect specific pathways involved in inflammation.
They may be considered for selected adults with severe, inadequately controlled chronic rhinosinusitis with nasal polyps, particularly when symptoms remain significant despite appropriate standard treatment or disease repeatedly returns after surgery.
Depending on the medicine and local approval, biologic treatment may improve nasal obstruction, smell, polyp burden, and the need for systemic corticosteroids or further surgery.
Eligibility varies according to the medicine, clinical guidelines, disease severity, coexisting conditions, and previous treatment response.
Biologics require specialist assessment and monitoring. They are not necessary for most people with mild nasal polyps.
7. Antibiotics
Antibiotics treat bacterial infections; they do not directly eliminate nasal polyps.
A clinician may prescribe antibiotics when there is sufficient evidence of a bacterial infection and treatment is considered appropriate.
Coloured nasal mucus alone does not establish that antibiotics are needed.
Unnecessary antibiotic use can cause side effects and contribute to antimicrobial resistance. The decision should be based on clinical findings rather than the presence of nasal polyps alone.
8. Endoscopic Sinus Surgery
Endoscopic sinus surgery may be considered when nasal polyps cause significant symptoms and appropriate medical treatment has not provided sufficient relief.
During the procedure, the surgeon uses an endoscope and specialised instruments to remove polyps and improve access to affected sinus openings.
The goal is to restore ventilation and drainage, reduce the burden of disease, and improve the delivery of topical medication to the sinus lining.
Surgery may improve nasal breathing and smell, but it does not remove the underlying tendency towards chronic inflammation.
Possible risks include bleeding, infection, recurrence, and uncommon injury to nearby structures, including those associated with the eyes or the base of the skull.
The exact risks depend on the extent of surgery and individual circumstances. The ENT surgeon should explain the expected benefits, alternatives, and potential complications before treatment.
9. Combined Treatment
Some patients benefit most from a combination of approaches.
For example, treatment may include saline irrigation and a corticosteroid nasal spray before surgery, followed by ongoing topical medication after the procedure.
Patients with asthma or allergic rhinitis may also require treatment for those conditions.
The purpose of combined care is to control symptoms while addressing the chronic inflammatory process that contributes to recurrence.
When Is Nasal Polyp Surgery Necessary?
Nasal polyp surgery is not required for everyone.
An ENT specialist may consider surgery when:
•	Nasal obstruction remains severe despite appropriate medical treatment.
•	Polyps substantially affect breathing or sleep.
•	The sense of smell remains significantly impaired.
•	Chronic rhinosinusitis continues to affect quality of life.
•	The disease repeatedly returns or remains difficult to control.
•	Imaging shows sinus disease that may benefit from surgical management.
•	The diagnosis is uncertain or a growth requires further evaluation.
The decision should be based on the full clinical picture rather than the size of a polyp alone.
Patients should ask about the likely benefits, recovery period, possible complications, alternatives, and the need for ongoing treatment after surgery.
Recovery After Nasal Polyp Surgery
Recovery varies according to the extent of surgery, individual health, and the severity of the underlying condition.
Many patients return home on the day of surgery, although some may need additional observation.
What to Expect After Surgery
Temporary nasal blockage, mild discomfort, blood-stained discharge, and crusting may occur during early recovery.
Swelling can make the nose feel blocked even after the polyps have been removed. Improvement may occur gradually as healing progresses.
Follow the surgeon's instructions regarding rest, medication, nasal irrigation, and activity.
Nasal Irrigation After Surgery
Saline irrigation is often recommended after endoscopic sinus surgery to help clear mucus and crusting.
The timing and frequency should follow the surgeon's instructions because the appropriate routine depends on the procedure and healing stage.
Use safe water and keep irrigation equipment clean.
Follow-Up Appointments
Follow-up appointments allow the ENT specialist to assess healing, remove crusting when necessary, and monitor for inflammation or recurrence.
Some patients may need additional nasal endoscopy during recovery.
Attend the recommended appointments even if breathing improves quickly.
When to Seek Medical Advice After Surgery
Contact the treating team if you develop increasing pain, fever, worsening swelling, persistent heavy bleeding, or other concerning symptoms.
Seek urgent medical care for heavy uncontrolled bleeding, changes in vision, severe headache, confusion, or clear watery drainage that the surgical team has identified as a warning sign.
Your discharge instructions should explain which symptoms require urgent assessment.
Can Nasal Polyps Come Back After Treatment?
Yes. Nasal polyps can recur after treatment, including after surgery.
This is because surgery removes the growths and improves access to the sinuses but does not necessarily eliminate the underlying inflammatory tendency.
The likelihood of recurrence varies according to disease severity, associated asthma, aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease, previous treatment history, and other factors.
Some people experience long periods of improvement, while others require ongoing treatment to keep symptoms under control.
How Can Recurrence Be Reduced?
A long-term management plan may include:
•	Using prescribed corticosteroid nasal sprays consistently.
•	Performing saline irrigation when recommended.
•	Attending ENT follow-up appointments.
•	Managing asthma and allergic rhinitis.
•	Avoiding known irritants when practical.
•	Reporting a gradual return of congestion or smell loss.
•	Discussing additional treatment if symptoms remain uncontrolled.
No strategy can guarantee that polyps will never return. However, ongoing management can help control inflammation and identify recurrence before symptoms become severe.
Nasal Polyps and Asthma: Understanding the Connection
Nasal polyps and asthma frequently occur together because both may involve chronic inflammation of the respiratory tract.
The nose, sinuses, and lungs are parts of the respiratory system, and inflammation affecting one area may coexist with disease in another.
People with both conditions may experience persistent nasal blockage, reduced smell, coughing, wheezing, or episodes of breathlessness.
When nasal polyps are identified in someone with asthma, clinicians may review how well the asthma is controlled.
Similarly, a person with asthma who develops persistent nasal symptoms or smell loss may benefit from assessment for chronic rhinosinusitis with nasal polyps.
If aspirin or certain anti-inflammatory medicines trigger respiratory symptoms, inform the healthcare professional. This history may suggest aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease and influence the treatment plan.
Coordinated care can help address both upper and lower airway symptoms.
Nasal Polyps in Children
Nasal obstruction in children can have several causes, including enlarged adenoids, allergic rhinitis, infections, and anatomical problems.
Nasal polyps are less common in children than in adults. When they are identified, the clinician may assess whether an underlying condition needs further investigation.
Symptoms may include persistent nasal blockage, mouth breathing, snoring, reduced smell, and recurring nasal discharge.
A one-sided nasal obstruction or an unusual nasal mass requires appropriate examination to determine the cause.
Treatment depends on the type of growth and the child's clinical condition. Medication or surgery may be considered according to specialist advice.
Parents should not use adult nasal medications or prolonged decongestant sprays in children without appropriate guidance.
Lifestyle Measures for Managing Nasal Polyps
Lifestyle changes cannot reliably cure nasal polyps, but certain practical steps may support symptom management and general respiratory health.
1. Reduce Exposure to Known Irritants
Smoke, dust, strong chemical fumes, and air pollution can irritate the respiratory tract.
Reducing exposure where practical may help some people manage nasal discomfort.
Avoiding irritants will not necessarily shrink existing polyps, but it can form part of a broader management plan.
2. Manage Allergies
If allergies contribute to symptoms, follow the treatment plan recommended by your clinician.
Practical measures may include reducing exposure to known allergens and keeping indoor spaces clean. The best approach depends on the allergen involved.
3. Maintain Safe Nasal Hygiene
Saline irrigation may help clear mucus and improve comfort when used correctly.
Use sterile, distilled, or previously boiled and cooled water for irrigation, and clean the device according to the manufacturer's instructions.
Do not share nasal irrigation equipment.
4. Avoid Excessive Use of Decongestant Sprays
Topical decongestant sprays may provide short-term relief, but prolonged or frequent use can cause rebound congestion.
This can create a cycle in which the nose becomes increasingly blocked when the medicine wears off.
Use decongestant sprays only according to the product instructions or clinician advice. They are not a long-term treatment for nasal polyps.
5. Follow Prescribed Treatment Consistently
Chronic inflammatory conditions often require regular management even when symptoms improve.
If a prescribed treatment is ineffective or causes side effects, discuss alternatives with your clinician rather than stopping treatment without advice.
Common Myths About Nasal Polyps
Misinformation about nasal polyps can lead to unnecessary worry or ineffective treatment. The following facts clarify several common misconceptions.
Myth 1: Nasal Polyps Are Cancerous
Fact: Most nasal polyps are benign inflammatory growths. However, an unusual or one-sided nasal mass needs medical assessment because other conditions can resemble a polyp.
Myth 2: Nasal Polyps Always Need Surgery
Fact: Many patients are managed with medication and saline irrigation. Surgery is considered when symptoms remain significant, medical treatment is insufficient, or further evaluation is needed.
Myth 3: Nasal Polyps Are Caused Only by Allergies
Fact: Allergic rhinitis may coexist with nasal polyps, but chronic inflammation can develop for several reasons. Not every patient has an identifiable allergy.
Myth 4: Surgery Permanently Cures Nasal Polyps
Fact: Surgery can improve airflow and sinus drainage, but the underlying inflammatory condition may persist. Ongoing treatment and follow-up may be necessary.
Myth 5: Antibiotics Can Eliminate Nasal Polyps
Fact: Antibiotics treat certain bacterial infections, not the polyps themselves. Treatment should be selected according to the clinical diagnosis.
Myth 6: Home Remedies Can Reliably Dissolve Nasal Polyps
Fact: No home remedy has been established as a reliable way to eliminate nasal polyps. Saline irrigation may relieve symptoms, but it should complement appropriate medical care.
When Should You See an ENT Specialist?
Consider consulting an ENT specialist if you experience:
•	Persistent nasal congestion lasting several weeks.
•	Reduced or lost sense of smell.
•	Repeated sinus symptoms or persistent nasal discharge.
•	Difficulty breathing through the nose.
•	Snoring or sleep disruption associated with nasal blockage.
•	Symptoms that do not improve with appropriate initial treatment.
•	Recurrent nasal polyps after previous treatment or surgery.
•	Nasal obstruction that affects everyday activities or quality of life.
Warning Signs That Need Prompt Assessment
A persistent one-sided nasal blockage, recurrent nosebleeds, a visible one-sided mass, facial numbness, or worsening facial pain should be assessed promptly.
Seek urgent medical attention if nasal or sinus symptoms occur with changes in vision, swelling around the eye, severe headache, confusion, or significant neurological symptoms.
These features may indicate a condition other than routine inflammatory nasal polyps and should not be ignored.
Frequently Asked Questions About Nasal Polyps
1. What are the first symptoms of nasal polyps?
Early symptoms may include persistent nasal congestion, a reduced sense of smell, postnasal drip, or difficulty breathing through the nose. Small polyps may cause no noticeable symptoms. A nasal examination is needed to confirm the diagnosis.
2. Can nasal polyps disappear on their own?
Some mild symptoms may improve as inflammation settles, but established nasal polyps do not reliably disappear without treatment. A clinician can determine whether medication, monitoring, or another approach is appropriate.
3. What is the most effective treatment for nasal polyps?
Intranasal corticosteroids and saline irrigation are common first-line treatments. Selected patients may benefit from short courses of oral corticosteroids, biologic medicines, or endoscopic sinus surgery. The most appropriate option depends on disease severity and individual circumstances.
4. Can nasal polyps cause loss of smell?
Yes. Nasal polyps and associated inflammation can interfere with the normal movement of odour molecules to the smell receptors. Treating the inflammation may improve smell, although recovery varies and is not guaranteed.
5. Are nasal polyps related to asthma?
Yes. Nasal polyps and asthma can occur together and may share inflammatory mechanisms. People with both conditions may need coordinated treatment to manage upper and lower airway symptoms.
6. Do nasal polyps cause headaches?
Some people with chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps experience facial pressure or headaches. However, headaches have many causes, and a headache alone does not establish that nasal polyps are present.
7. Is nasal polyp surgery painful?
Discomfort after surgery varies. Patients may experience temporary congestion, mild pain, or blood-stained discharge during recovery. The surgical team will explain expected symptoms and recommend appropriate pain management.
8. How long does recovery from nasal polyp surgery take?
Recovery depends on the extent of surgery and individual healing. Nasal congestion and crusting may persist during the early recovery period, with improvement occurring gradually. Follow-up appointments help the surgeon assess healing and guide ongoing treatment.
9. Can nasal polyps return after surgery?
Yes. Recurrence is possible because surgery does not necessarily eliminate the underlying inflammation. Regular use of prescribed treatment and appropriate follow-up may help control symptoms and manage recurrence.
10. Can nasal polyps be prevented?
There is no guaranteed way to prevent nasal polyps. Managing chronic rhinosinusitis, asthma, and allergies where present may help control associated inflammation. Early assessment of persistent symptoms can support timely treatment.
11. Are nasal polyps contagious?
No. Nasal polyps themselves are not contagious. They are inflammatory growths rather than an infection that spreads from person to person.
12. Can nasal polyps affect sleep?
Yes. Nasal obstruction may contribute to snoring, mouth breathing, and disturbed sleep. Persistent sleep problems should be evaluated because other conditions, including obstructive sleep apnoea, may also be involved.
Conclusion
Nasal polyps are common non-cancerous growths associated with chronic inflammation of the nasal and sinus lining. Although they are usually benign, persistent nasal obstruction, reduced smell, postnasal drip, and recurring sinus symptoms can affect daily comfort, sleep, and quality of life.
Understanding nasal polyp symptoms and seeking an appropriate diagnosis are important first steps. An ENT specialist may use nasal examination, endoscopy, and selected imaging investigations to determine the extent of the condition and rule out other causes of nasal obstruction.
Available nasal polyp treatments range from saline irrigation and corticosteroid nasal sprays to selected oral medicines, biologic therapies, and endoscopic sinus surgery. The right approach depends on the severity of symptoms, associated conditions, and response to previous treatment.
Because nasal polyps can recur, long-term management may be necessary even after successful surgery. Regular follow-up, consistent use of prescribed treatment, and appropriate management of asthma or allergies can help support better symptom control.
If you experience persistent nasal congestion, unexplained smell loss, or recurring sinus problems, consider seeking medical evaluation rather than relying solely on temporary remedies. Early assessment can help identify the cause of your symptoms and determine a suitable treatment plan.
Most importantly, unusual one-sided nasal symptoms, repeated bleeding, changes in vision, or severe facial pain should be assessed promptly. A personalised, evidence-based approach offers the best foundation for managing nasal polyps and maintaining long-term nasal and sinus health.
